#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e094fc-2b61-5865-923d-4763475d0f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698494} {:answer/id #uuid "3634df59-c3ad-5cf9-9602-a7baafddf944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698498} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac146da-93a2-5706-9523-806b23f06ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y^2+3z^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698502} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7ce3b3-a3e3-5e75-85e2-10184c81d07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "xy^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698512} {:answer/id #uuid "f7277870-6275-5868-944b-d733c4d0b61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698498} :answer-field/id #uuid "e099f454-d439-59b1-bb8f-af9c2bda14b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c701d949-2d48-5058-b224-6149f2ad0e9c" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+z^3.$ Treat $x$ and $z$ as constants." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ and $z$ fixed. The factor $x$ is a constant multiplier, and $z^3$ is independent of $y$.\n\nUsing the sum, constant multiple, and power rules,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(y^2)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(z^3)\\\\\n&=x(2y)+0\\\\\n&=2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "174261a3-49f9-5d71-a822-ae2e918ce97f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698608} {:answer/id #uuid "810cd5e8-f414-5a92-aaf4-ff375c3cf0ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698614} {:answer/id #uuid "8249a94d-dd15-573d-94be-39dd28675fc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2\\sin x+3y^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698615} {:answer/id #uuid "f5732705-48ff-5966-a7bf-abac5da5b644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y(2x\\sin x-x^2\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698626} {:answer/id #uuid "f753b22d-5a8b-5ba3-a41a-8e44964010d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2y\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698627}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698608} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2155e07-23ea-53b2-84bd-b12e81bb2482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "33a806e4-8348-5f3d-a738-c2a4c7316b98" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y)=x^2y\\sin x+y^3.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. The term $y^3$ therefore has derivative zero, and $y$ is a constant multiplier in the first term:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}=y\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2\\sin x)+0.\n$$\n\nApply the product rule to $x^2\\sin x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y\\left(\\frac{\\partial x^2}{\\partial x}\\sin x+x^2\\frac{\\partial\\sin x}{\\partial x}\\right)\\\\\n&=y(2x\\sin x+x^2\\cos x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth product-rule terms are needed; differentiating both factors and multiplying their derivatives would omit them."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "238be668-7823-57de-be51-329d877b48ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698610} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dbdab2-be99-58ca-8c87-4e780c48f214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6e^2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698622} {:answer/id #uuid "eed0bfce-e7b6-5e3e-b8e4-797408805b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e^2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698623} {:answer/id #uuid "f2129ab0-aa2d-5a8a-bd7a-ad80af9cacdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5e^2+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698624} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7f9487-6e6e-5c2a-9033-70c7da289b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8e^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698623} :answer-field/id #uuid "d92dbf57-9aa3-54f0-a480-2f122b9e327d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "477db031-06da-516b-adcf-00a254df15d0" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=(x+y^2)(e^{xy}+\\ln y)$, evaluate $f_y(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Treat $x$ as constant. Both grouped factors depend on $y$, so neither entire factor is a constant multiplier. Let\n\n$$\nu=x+y^2,\\qquad v=e^{xy}+\\ln y.\n$$\n\nTheir partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2y,\\\\\nv_y&=e^{xy}\\frac{\\partial(xy)}{\\partial y}+\\frac{1}{y}\\\\\n&=xe^{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product rule gives\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y)=2y(e^{xy}+\\ln y)+(x+y^2)\\left(xe^{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}\\right).\n$$\n\nAt $(2,1)$, we have $\\ln1=0$ and $e^{xy}=e^2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=2(e^2+0)+(2+1)(2e^2+1)\\\\\n&=2e^2+6e^2+3\\\\\n&=8e^2+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $\\ln1=0$, its derivative contributes $1/y=1$ at this point. That contribution must be included before evaluating."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1feb66dc-0e3e-57c6-bc0f-5df061c100f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^2-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698609} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4925cf-e84b-5c71-bf73-2e7d57e4aa2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698611} {:answer/id #uuid "8eae04ad-4b30-5786-acae-5b51c7348cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^2-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698617} {:answer/id #uuid "90b41532-e733-588b-baf6-8ce16cee5ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^2-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698618} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dee8a2-322f-5729-8716-455cbef8f263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e^2-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698617} :answer-field/id #uuid "868ce9f1-43d9-560b-8c07-b063a4531e2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e82fda8f-936b-543f-af1e-7c2018d69d09" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=ye^{xy}-x^2y^2$, evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, keeping $x$ constant. Use the product rule on $ye^{xy}$ and the constant multiple rule on $x^2y^2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=1\\cdot e^{xy}+y\\frac{\\partial e^{xy}}{\\partial y}-2x^2y.\n$$\n\nThe exponential also requires the chain rule. Since $\\dfrac{\\partial(xy)}{\\partial y}=x$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{xy}+y e^{xy}x-2x^2y\\\\\n&=(1+xy)e^{xy}-2x^2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=(1+2\\cdot1)e^{2\\cdot1}-2\\cdot2^2\\cdot1\\\\\n&=3e^2-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the $y$ dependence until after differentiating. Substituting both coordinates first would leave only a number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a7f7b98-c79a-5983-b12c-da88cba3a2e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698732} {:answer/id #uuid "33085491-5312-5880-bfc6-2e1e4aca7ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698736} {:answer/id #uuid "6418be5f-57ec-5689-a0d1-6f776b32fe9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698741} {:answer/id #uuid "9b35a724-ced3-5fcd-8a7a-5cda7b18808f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e^2+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698749} {:answer/id #uuid "b52c53a6-cf04-520c-b26b-5d677fabd8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1+e^2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698741} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ed7e65-407c-5069-8aec-1ecb092fba7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16539140-b501-5199-9889-a77182c5ed32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+e^2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698730} {:answer/id #uuid "594a76e0-bc89-5d67-92a1-21108d0a2a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698738} {:answer/id #uuid "825fa4d8-277d-5503-9523-903802eb97b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698743} {:answer/id #uuid "849aad88-9478-55c1-b278-789dad2174a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698744} {:answer/id #uuid "b4326e83-efdf-5195-962e-a471fca02bbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e^2+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698743} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed3a4c0c-8e0a-5c4b-8610-bd1a60f94523" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "17c05b7d-1ccb-5232-b562-f1c6a1350d59" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\ln(1+e^{xy})+x^2y,\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,2)$.\n\n$f_x(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,2)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For $f_x$, treat $y$ as a constant. The logarithmic term requires the chain rule twice: first through the logarithm, then through the exponential. The term $x^2y$ uses the constant multiple rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^{xy}}\\cdot e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(xy)+2xy\\\\\n&=\\frac{ye^{xy}}{1+e^{xy}}+2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $f_y$, instead treat $x$ as a constant. In particular, the inner derivative of $xy$ is now $x$, and the derivative of $x^2y$ is $x^2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^{xy}}\\cdot e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy)+x^2\\\\\n&=\\frac{xe^{xy}}{1+e^{xy}}+x^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate each derivative at $x=1$, $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,2)&=\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+2\\cdot1\\cdot2=\\frac{2e^2}{1+e^2}+4,\\\\\nf_y(1,2)&=\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1^2=\\frac{e^2}{1+e^2}+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe two values differ because a different variable is held constant in each derivative. Also, $1+e^{xy}>0$ for all real $x,y$, so the logarithm and both derivatives are defined at the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698737 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27079040-9976-5028-a598-c9a13cb1c241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698734} {:answer/id #uuid "8c02e19b-a672-5399-a73f-4e88af59a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698746} {:answer/id #uuid "b70837f4-0cc7-5a81-8336-f19abc182d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698752} {:answer/id #uuid "ba02dc8d-c2fe-5a4f-9f53-39fafc06a96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698753} {:answer/id #uuid "bcdfe945-fc68-578b-b7f1-0e1f434bed20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698754} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a0afec-f5a9-5a9d-a090-993878ceb209" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39e13e50-9616-558b-a2da-0689d5cb4c25" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=e^{xy}+x^2y$, evaluate $f_y(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. Use the sum rule, then the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(e^{xy})+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2y)\\\\\n&=e^{xy}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy)+x^2\\cdot\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial y}\\\\\n&=xe^{xy}+x^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=2$ and $y=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,0)&=2e^{2\\cdot0}+2^2\\\\\n&=2\\cdot1+4\\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative is taken before evaluating; substituting both coordinates into $f$ first would leave only a number."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a5e10c6-e429-5ca6-960f-475d509588db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{\\ln(x+y^2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698735} {:answer/id #uuid "5c75f4ee-ae6f-5082-bd4e-54296e1b9e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y(x+y^2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698739} {:answer/id #uuid "5f9974fc-484a-5d80-95b3-f5441c58a0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{x+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698740} {:answer/id #uuid "98410e48-6d17-5aab-bab6-15d33aac0bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698748} {:answer/id #uuid "e81f122d-c156-5c5b-a328-610f95a657ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{x+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698740} :answer-field/id #uuid "939c9344-a3d5-56ea-8c6c-612a0088fdb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8bdc87f1-f622-5252-80a6-1d3652c87043" :question/problem "Find $f_y(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\ln(x+y^2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. Let $u=x+y^2$. Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}u}\\ln u=\\frac{1}{u},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=0+2y.\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x+y^2}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x+y^2)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{x+y^2}\\cdot 2y\\\\\n&=\\frac{2y}{x+y^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe formula applies where $x+y^2>0$, the domain of the logarithm."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2621e07c-5376-55e5-8cbe-302266f28c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698760} {:answer/id #uuid "2e29c9d0-d84b-5149-901e-003ad0823432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698762} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3ff607-b55e-51f4-9e23-51a1b0e12aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698773} {:answer/id #uuid "caae950d-8ead-5c06-b75c-9caeb8e64350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698775} {:answer/id #uuid "f561e67f-b76c-5d17-93fb-eb5b44189b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698762} :answer-field/id #uuid "30058bce-34f1-536c-b32f-4adde94f543f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "908d866d-c5d2-5fac-9d5d-89bc64f4361a" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^2-y^2}{e^{y-x}}$, evaluate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(1,0)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant. In particular, the chain rule gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}e^{y-x}=-e^{y-x}.\n$$\n\nApply the quotient rule before evaluating at the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\n&=\\frac{2x e^{y-x}-(x^2-y^2)(-e^{y-x})}{(e^{y-x})^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2x+x^2-y^2}{e^{y-x}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(1,0)}\n=\\frac{2+1-0}{e^{-1}}=3e.\n$$\n\nThe denominator is always positive, so the function and this derivative are defined at the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "238115dd-b58b-5e08-94d5-b7da915bda70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl((x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698759} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcec15b-23b1-5788-becc-ee1d0c36a87c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl(x(x+y^2)+1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698761} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef07d99-eb44-5adb-850e-20c77cf3ad3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{x+y^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698763} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3c65db-f38e-5e5f-a132-ad0dfce469b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{xy^2}\\bigl(y^2(x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698765} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6ee467-6f19-54ee-9b74-e70055683cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698769}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698769} :answer-field/id #uuid "9920d21b-94b2-53d3-9ac4-48f700e9964b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9537a10a-ace7-5581-a0a1-2a2907755cd0" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{e^{xy^2}}{x+y^2},\n$$\n\nfind $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$, where $x+y^2\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant. The numerator contains a composite function, so use the chain rule to differentiate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}e^{xy^2}\n&=e^{xy^2}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy^2)=2xy e^{xy^2}, \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x+y^2)&=2y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth numerator and denominator depend on $y$. Apply the quotient rule, preserving the subtraction and the squared denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{(2xy e^{xy^2})(x+y^2)-e^{xy^2}(2y)}{(x+y^2)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{2y e^{xy^2}\\bigl(x(x+y^2)-1\\bigr)}{(x+y^2)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $2y e^{xy^2}$ is common to the numerator terms; it cannot be canceled with the sum $x+y^2$ in the denominator. The derivative is defined wherever $x+y^2\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698774 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f3d54d-ab52-50bb-9399-11584f498a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x-12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698757} {:answer/id #uuid "1b082d39-9ba2-5cc7-bffc-0f946a61987a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698758} {:answer/id #uuid "71bdaab4-8266-50e2-9b5c-b716cd1fac86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698766} {:answer/id #uuid "7572906f-f58e-533c-8832-a74b080fe0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698767} {:answer/id #uuid "862708d0-5d80-5d64-bcf2-b3ab61244dfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x-6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698758} :answer-field/id #uuid "e565c205-0d11-58fe-a260-964a57426bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "acb99466-30f7-53aa-bbea-e3163ae8199e" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2x+3y}{x-2y}$. The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(x-2y)^2}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant. The numerator and denominator derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2x+3y)=3,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x-2y)=-2.\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\n&=\\frac{3(x-2y)-(2x+3y)(-2)}{(x-2y)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3x-6y+4x+6y}{(x-2y)^2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{7x}{(x-2y)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing with the supplied form gives $h(x,y)=7x$. These formulas apply where $x-2y\\ne0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016082c2-218d-5e91-ad3b-67a4635ea744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698812} {:answer/id #uuid "44e5916a-7269-59f5-ab94-7088efdcef15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698822} {:answer/id #uuid "489b976a-77c2-5f06-97a5-7db9eff8c574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(x + z\\right) \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698823} {:answer/id #uuid "49323d5f-361e-5fc9-95fc-30c552256e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698824} {:answer/id #uuid "a32016a8-7dba-59fc-a28f-a90af29a3ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698840}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698812} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6a9550-9bc7-57e5-8c2a-6b2653233356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186698845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4bc73b47-76fa-5aa8-bdf3-2b3740b5a48f" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right) \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} + \\left(y + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to $y$, holding $x$ and $z$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $y$; all remaining factors stay fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\left(x + z\\right)\\cos y+\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\cdot 1\\\\&=\\left(x + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186698826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008f